            <ArticleTitle>An undetected intraorbital foreign body after a &quot;trivial&quot; facial injury.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1782-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/SCS.0000000000000905</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Intraorbital foreign objects are common after high-velocity injuries and can result in disastrous consequences. A &quot;trivial&quot; facial injury may sometimes obscure the presence of an intraorbital foreign object. Difficulties in the approach to the intraconal space and possible surgical morbidity to the optic nerve make management especially challenging. We are reporting a case wherein an intraconal foreign body was missed during the initial examination of a maxillofacial injury and was subsequently successfully extracted by an endoscopic approach.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
